<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basque Nationalist Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as PNV, EAJ-PNV, Euzko Alderdi Jeltzalea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basque Nationalist Party is a Christian democratic party founded in 1895 by Sabino Arana to defend Basque identity and self-government. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it sided with the Republic in return for autonomy, and its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-aguirre">José Antonio Aguirre</a> headed the Basque government of 1936–1937 and then a government in exile. It has led the Basque autonomous government for most of the period since 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basque-nationalist-party.jpg" alt="Basque Nationalist Party" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Euzko Alderdi Jeltzalea-Partido Nacionalista Vasco · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_PNV_2025.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded in 1895 by Sabino de Arana Goiri as a Catholic and conservative party that agitated for the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> and the defence of Basque culture and language. Its early ideology rested on an essentialist idea of the Basque nation, Catholic integrism, notions of racial purity and opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, socialism and industrialisation, and membership was at first limited to those who could show eight Basque surnames. By 1897 it sought independence for all seven provinces of the Basque Country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its motto, God and the Old Laws, refers to the fueros, the traditional laws of the Basque provinces, and Basque nationalism grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlism">Carlism</a>.</p><p>In 1916 the party renamed itself the Basque Nationalist Communion and sought autonomy within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> rather than independence. The independence-minded Aberrianos were expelled in 1921 and reclaimed the old name. The party was outlawed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>, from 1923 to 1930, and the two groups reunited at the end of 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> began in July 1936 the party was torn: it sympathised with the rebels' Catholic and anti-communist side, but the rebels insisted on a unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and many of them were fascists. The Republic secured its allegiance with the promise of a Basque Autonomy Statute, and the Biscayan and Gipuzkoan branches declared for the Republic. In October 1936 the party leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-aguirre">José Antonio Aguirre</a> became the first lehendakari of the Basque Government. Guernica was bombed by German aircraft in April 1937, and in July 1937 the Basque Army surrendered to Italian troops under the Santoña Agreement. The government then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and later into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The presidents of the Basque Government in exile were always party members. In 1959 the group that became ETA was created by young people who were disgruntled at the party's ineffective action. After Franco's death in 1975 the party took part in the new order: it urged abstention in the 1978 referendum on the Spanish Constitution, which it said lacked Basque input, but supported the Statute of Autonomy approved in December 1978. Carlos Garaikoetxea led the first autonomous government, and in 1987 dissenters formed Eusko Alkartasuna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party operates across the Basque Autonomous Community, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, Treviño and the French Basque Country. Its meeting places are called batzokis, and over 200 exist throughout the world. A member is a jelkide. It celebrates Aberri Eguna on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, as it has since 1932, and Alderdi Eguna, Party Day, on the last Sunday of September, with a political meeting, stands, music, dances and traditional sports. Its National Council, the Euzkadi-Buru-Batzar, was created in 1911.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basque Nationalist Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>José Antonio Aguirre of the <b>Basque Nationalist Party</b> was sworn in as first lehendakari under the oak of Guernica on 7 October 1936.</blockquote><a href="/state/euzkadi-1936-1937">Euzkadi (1936-1937)</a></li></ul></section>
